=== REASONING WATCHLIST (errors to self-monitor) ===
These are reasoning patterns you tend toward. Self-monitor — if you catch yourself using one, flag it explicitly rather than letting it pass.
- [skp-beliefs-171] Claims That AI Benefits Outweigh Harms Lack Falsifiable Criteria: Nirvana fallacy — The node implicitly compares existing benefit claims against an ideal of perfect falsifiability rather than against a realistic standard of what policy-relevant social science claims can achieve.
- [skp-intentions-136] Pinning AI Safety Liability to Commercial Infrastructure Executives: Single Point Accountability Fallacy — The node assumes that assigning a named individual resolves diffuse organizational responsibility, but for complex multi-team AI releases, no single individual can genuinely comprehend or control all risk vectors, making the accountability assignment nominally meaningful but practically hollow.
- [skp-intentions-110] Establishing Decentralized User-Controlled Cryptographic Receipts for Algorithmic Discovery: Availability Heuristic / Techno-Legal Solutionism — The proposal treats cryptographic logging and statutory discovery as sufficient accountability mechanisms, potentially overstating the practical value of telemetry access when the underlying interpretability problem remains unsolved.
- [skp-intentions-131] Require Worker-Led Safety Vetoes Backed by Mechanistic Interpretability Evidence: Nirvana fallacy — The proposal implicitly contrasts worker-led mechanistic audits against corporate red-teaming and government gatekeeping as if the former is unproblematically superior, without accounting for the real limitations of mechanistic interpretability tools and worker access in practice.
- [skp-beliefs-170] AI Corporations Systematically Evade Effective Regulation: Hasty Generalization — Drawing a 'systematic' pattern of evasion from a set of documented but selective cases (audit washing, compute thresholds, acquihires) risks overgeneralizing to the entire AI corporate sector without accounting for firms with substantive compliance efforts.
- [skp-intentions-105] Require Public Oversight Bodies to Verify AI Alignment via Mechanistic Interpretability: Nirvana Fallacy — The node implicitly contrasts imperfect existing oversight mechanisms (behavioral red-teaming, centralized agencies, market liability) against an idealized mechanistic interpretability regime, without adequately accounting for the practical limitations of the proposed alternative.
- [skp-intentions-105] Require Public Oversight Bodies to Verify AI Alignment via Mechanistic Interpretability: Premature Universalization — The call for 'universal engineering standards' and federated protocols presupposes that mechanistic interpretability methods are generalizable across architectures and scales, a premise not currently supported by the research literature.
- [skp-beliefs-164] Companies Use Narrow Audits to Deflect Meaningful AI Oversight: Motive attribution fallacy — The claim presupposes intentional deception or deflection as the dominant corporate motive, conflating the observable outcome (narrow audit scope) with a specific bad-faith intent, when resource constraints, legal liability concerns, or immature standards could produce identical behavior.
- [skp-intentions-143] Agentic Authority Demarcation: False precision — The intention assumes that AI authority can be cleanly 'demarcated' into defined scopes, but the emergent and context-dependent behavior of AI agents resists the kind of crisp boundary-drawing that legal and governance frameworks typically require.
- [skp-intentions-008] Replace Architectural Pre-Clearance with Purpose-Bound Data Licenses: False Dichotomy — The node frames the choice as binary between 'architectural pre-clearance' and 'purpose-bound data licenses,' obscuring the possibility that both mechanisms, or hybrid approaches, might be necessary for comprehensive oversight.

